If not a better life in this world people had hope that a God would lead them to a better life hereafter in an eternally pleasant heaven, or the equivalent of heaven. This prospect is a soothing and comforting thought when death is imminent; for the person dying and for their friends and relatives. People are more accepting of extreme hardships in this life with the knowledge of an eternally pleasant afterlife. Some have even been persuaded to kill themselves, or to kill others with the belief God wants this and will reward them in an afterlife.

A God has been the supreme source of appeal for help and support when the going gets tough. God is universally accessible - anyone can pray or appeal to Him through the right rituals. God is more available than any convenience store. HE is always open for business, 24/7, bank holidays included. HE is also quiet and unobtrusive. This is definitely so, have you ever heard or seen him?

To date God offers:

a constant listening service;
security and comfort;
forgiveness and the prospect of a clean slate;
hope and convenience;
a source of final arbitration;
an afterlife of eternal bliss.

For believers in the rewards of a godly life death would be just a staging post. It would not have the finality and generally unpleasant emptiness of a godless universe.

Ultimate Comfort Blanket

[Figure: The Comfort Blanket will help patch things up: a patchwork quilt (Metropolitan Museum of Art)]

With all these benefits God(s) became the ultimate comfort blanket. This was in addition to His much-wanted other key role; as a safety deposit box. These two attributes are wholly in keeping with Maslow (1908 - 1970), the eminent American psychologist, Hierarchy of Needs, usually shown as a pyramid. They fit neatly into his second tier; i.e. security. The evolutionary advantage of group spiritual conformity matches with Maslow's row three; i.e. belonging.

They wanted to demonstrate there is a God by offering one or more proofs. These purported proofs can be split into two categories: (1) religious texts and (2) logical proofs.

Religious texts: Their very existence is pointed to by many of the faithful as proof of God and justification for their religion. Absolutism, ie. these writings are unchallengeable, is often claimed for various holy texts such as the: Bible (Christianity); Qur'an (Islam); Tanakh, of which the Torah is a part, (Judaism); sacred Vedas (Hinduism); Sutras (Buddhism) and Guru Granth Sahib (Sikhism). There are others.

[Figure: Four scriptures: the King James Bible (1611), the Aleppo Codex of the Hebrew Bible, the Birmingham Qur'an and the Guru Granth Sahib]

The texts, particularly relating to the Abrahamic God, have often been disputed by unbelievers. In response to these challenges some past and present theologians have come up with a number of attempted Logical Proofs. These, they believe, give credence to the claim that God exists.

Logical proofs: Aside from sacred texts there are six main arguments made for God's existence, mnemonic for these = COTHAM:

Cosmological;
Ontological;
Teleological;
Hard-wired;
Anthropic Principle;
Moralistic theory

You won't find these six purported proofs neatly packaged together in any Google or Wiki search, but they certainly can be found individually in the vast body of literature on this subject.

All these attempted proofs are just that, attempts. They are not proofs. They demonstrate evidence of creative thought, but not evidence of the creator.

More detail on these in the next chapter.

He went on to say that just because we can imagine it does not mean the thing exists in reality. Aquinas also dismissed the concept (points scoring? - see philosophers' chapter) so did Immanuel Kant. The ontological argument has survived nearly 1,000 years. It continues to be debated right up to the present day. It was discussed on BBC Radio 4 programme "In Our Time" in 2012.

http://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/b01mwx64

Teleological Argument

This is also known as argument by design. In essence, it is claimed that the world and every plant and animal is so perfect it could only have come about by the expressed design of God. William Paley (1743-1805) put the case quite succinctly in his book Natural Theology.

[Figure: Image: Title page "NATURAL THEOLOGY: OR, EVIDENCES OF THE EXISTENCE AND ATTRIBUTES OF THE DEITY, COLLECTED FROM THE APPEARANCES OF NATURE, BY WILLIAM PALEY, D.D. PHILADELPHIA:"]

[Figure: William Paley (1743-1805), after George Romney]

Paley imagined someone finding a watch and reasoned:

• The existence of the watch is proof of a watchmaker;
• The watch could not have come about by accident (unlike a rock or stone);
• The universe is intricate in its design (e.g. molecular structure of ice, the position of the Earth from the Sun etc.);
• The universe could not come about by accident;
• The universe itself is proof of an intelligent designer (but needs a bit more of a super designer than the watch).

The teleological argument is a favourite of the Intelligent Design people. Plants and animals, including humans, are perfectly adapted to their environment, through God's design. Thus, claim the IDers. David Hume argued that the apparently ordered world could come about by chance. Darwin, assisted by Mendel, put meat on the bone by showing that the adaptation of flora and fauna has come about through hundreds of millions of years of evolutionary honing.

We now think that she, or he, is much more likely to have looked like this:

[Figure: Reconstruction of a Neanderthal head (J. H. McGregor, American Museum of Natural History, 1915)]

from a find at La Chapelle-aux-Saints, France, in 1908. The re-construction above was made by J. H. McGregor for the American Museum of Natural History in 1915; modern ones, made by scientists using sophisticated techniques, look even more like us.

Our updated understanding has also turned on its head some earlier statements about Neanderthals. We now have much more knowledge of Neanderthals, their anatomy, intelligence and how they lived. We have moved well beyond the studies made by B and S in the later part of the 19th century. There have been detailed examinations of hundreds of Neanderthal sites. Some major ones include Shanidar in Iraq; Kebara in Israel; Krapina in Croatia; Combe-Grenal and Abri in France. As a result of all these finds and use of modern means of examination some comparisons can now be accurately made between Neanderthals and MH:

They were generally 13cm (6") shorter;
Their average brain size was very slightly larger than MH (size is not necessarily synonymous with thinking power);
Neanderthals were of stockier build and had heavier facial features, with a sloping brow;
Like MH they were artists, but were much older artists with Neanderthal cave paintings, crude animal and lines, discovered in the Maltravieso Cave, Spain. They are dated 64,000 years old.

MH come in a wide variety of shapes, heights and colours. If a Neanderthal was brought forward to modern times, shaved, given a good haircut and put in jeans and a polo shirt, he would not look out of place on the South Bank in London.

Neanderthals were the dominant bi-pedal species in Europe for about 200,000 years, during the Middle Paleolithic. But once MH began moving into the continent around 45,000 years ago their numbers began to wane. Neanderthals eventually died out about 40,000 years ago. The last out-post is thought to have been Gibraltar. However, there is some evidence to suggest they may have survived longer in sub-arctic Russia, at Byzovaya. At their peak it's estimated that there were 70,000.

All early civilisations have left something behind to tell the modern world what they were about. There are magnificent ruined buildings, art objects, tombs and written work - sometimes on stone. They may have demonstrated expertise in various fields, like astronomy. Altogether though they don't get anywhere near the impact ancient Greece has had. No archaic civilisation has so firmly and widely imprinted itself on modern life as ancient Greece.

Here's what we owe them:

Language - at least 500 English words come from Greek. This is important because English is a common world language, generally used in commerce, science and politics;

Democracy - Athenian men would gather equally on a hill, the Pnyx, to talk and decide on laws, justice and who should be exiled. This was the start of democracy, not for women though! The Greeks also originated the name. Democratic government is now seen as the fairest form of government in the Western World and in some other parts. Several departments of the United Nations actively support the world-wide establishment of democracy and democratic government;

Math's, Science, Logic: Some of the best-known mathematicians and scientists, among many, are: Archimedes who calculated the arc of a parabola and an accurate version of Pi. His 'Archimedes Screw' for transporting liquids and other substances is still used today; Democritus computed the volume of a cylinder and proposed the atom as the smallest indivisible substance; Eratosthenes invented latitude and longitude in his work on geography; Euclid is known as the father of geometry; Hipparchus is thought to be the founder of trigonometry and discovered the precession (rotation) of equinoxes; Pythagoras introduced his famous theorem of the right-angled triangle. Aristotle, the great polymath, proposed formal rules for Logical analysis.

Medicine - Hippocrates was a great physician and known as the Father of Medicine. His ethical standards were enshrined in the Hippocratic Oath, still used today, with amended wording;

Philosophy - challenging accepted thinking was a new concept to ancient people. Usually the accepted norm was so because it had powerful support, sometimes of the deadly variety, from governance or the Gods. The Greeks by discovering philosophy originated this philosophical exploration of reality. The list of groundbreaking Greek philosophers is too numerous to put down here. Thales started them off, as the founder of western philosophy. Much later there was Socrates, claimed the wisest and possibly the most annoying man in Greece. A

little later came Plato. Whitehead said the whole of western philosophy was a series of footnotes to Plato;

Architecture - the remnants of iconic ancient buildings still remain. The Parthenon and Temple of Zeus in Athens are but two of many to inspire more modern architecture. To present arts, sport and theatre the Greeks designed amphitheaters, on which many people could stand and sit on a circular, raised dais. These were the precursors to modern stadia and indoor theatres. The Hellenic Greek style of columns is still copied, more than 2,500 years after their original introduction: Doric, Ionic and Corinthian;

Literature and Entertainment - What a lineup of talented writers those ancient Greeks had, starting around 2,700 years ago, with Homer and Hesiod. These two wrote entertaining and poetic pieces of myths and Gods. They laid the foundation for Greece's ancient religious beliefs as the myths and Gods became believable. Their works have also been the base for other ancient Greek writers. In modern times the myths invented by Homer and Hesiod's have been used in stories and films. Here are some: Hercules; Jason and the Argonauts, Clash of the Titans, The Immortals, Helen of Troy, the 1987 TV series The Odyssey and others. We've also enjoyed, through film, the adventures of Perseus, the hero who killed the monster Medusa, a snake head Gorgon. Apart from Homer and Hesiod we also have Aesop, the black slave of 620 to 560 BCE. He gave us his witty and pointed fables. Many of these offered moral advice. Their phraseology has become part of the English language with terms such as: "crying wolf"; "the lion's share", "sour grapes" and "dog in a manger". One of the best known is the tale of the hare and the tortoise. The fables may have originally been in oral rather than written form. There's Sappho with her lyrical poetry, Aeschylus, the dramatist and originator, known in the theatre world as the father of tragedy. He wrote Agamemnon among 90 plays. Sophocles was another playwright who wrote more than 120 plays, including Ajax and Electra. Aristophanes was yet another playwright, but not a writer of tragedies. He was possibly the first writer of comedy that we know of. His most famous surviving play is Lysistrata. This is about Greek women coming together to deny men sex, unless the Peloponnesian War is ended. Finally, we have the third great ancient Greek tragedian, Euripides. He introduced a new approach by casting his mythical characters as relatively ordinary people caught up in extraordinary circumstances. His influence extends to the present day. He also shocked ancient Greek audiences by making his characters break accepted norms, e.g. his heroine, Medea, says:

"Sooner would I stand
Three times to face their battles, shield in hand,
Than bear one child!"

Art - development in Greek art saw the human body depicted in more realistic ways, this had influence on all art that followed. Despite her missing arms, the Venus de Milo in the Louvre Museum is rated one the best sculptures in the world. It's thought to have been chiseled by Alexandros of Antioch about 2,100 years ago;

History - in 425 BCE the Greek Herodotus showed the way to all subsequent historians. His immense work on the Greco-Persian wars, was a meticulous and systematic account. It explored the cause and effect of these conflicts in an analytic fashion. Herodotus showed all later historians how to write up on history;

Sport - they invented the greatest mass sports event of modern times, the Olympic Games. Just as they did, we hold this event every four years. They started up events like javelin, discus, marathon and long jump. Greeks also liked boxing and horse racing. Greek athletes and keep-fitters worked out in a Gymnasium. Today we still use this type of torture chamber - it is for some of us. But we shorten the name to Gym;

Wonders of the world - of the seven wonders of the world five were constructed wholly or partly by Greeks between 550 and 280 BCE: The Temple of Artemis; Statue of Zeus at Olympia; Mausoleum at Halicarnassus; Colossus of Rhodes and the Lighthouse of Alexandria.

Greek Religion: Homer and Hesiod, put a big oar in here, as previously mentioned. They were aided by some transcription from the preceding Minoans and Mycenaeans. The Greeks endowed a great deal to our modern world, but not with their religion, that is, except obliquely. The highly entertaining, but unbelievable antics of Greek Gods helped to inspire new thinking in the way of metaphysical philosophy. It could be said Greek religion kick-started philosophy.

The religion was quite a mixture of mythology, heroes, rituals and beliefs. There were a number of Gods and Goddesses. The top twelve Gods resided on high, on Mount Olympus. Of fourteen possible contenders, they are generally regarded to be:

[Figure: A Quakers' meeting, London, 1809 (Rowlandson and Pugin)]

These are also known as the Friends, or more completely the Religious Society of Friends or even the Friends Church. "You are my friends if you do what I command you" (John 15:14). Their founder was George Fox in the middle of the 17th century. Allegedly he told a magistrate to tremble at the name of God. Hence the name Quakers. Worldwide there are over 200,000 Quakers with 17,000 in Britain. For Quakers, every human has something of God within them, despite we can all think of people where he must be hiding extremely well inside of them, and never seems to appear.

Their key beliefs are:

God's light is in everyone;
God, this light, is pure love;
Someone who allows the light to guide their life will achieve complete relationship with God;
People do not need the intervention of a minister or priest. They can have a direct relationship with God;
Both redemption and the Kingdom of Heaven can be experience now, in this world.

Quakers do not celebrate Christian festivals like Christmas and Easter. Differently to other Christian sects, Quakers do not see the Bible as being the actual word of God. Other books can guide their lives. Quaker meetings are silent until someone, anyone attending, stands up and speaks. Quakers are focused on peace, human rights and living simply in the world.

Unitarians

[Figure: Joseph Priestley (1733 to 1804), English Unitarian]

There are quite a few differences between the big two. Arguably, the main disputes are about the Bible and the Pope. The Catholic Bible includes the 16 books of the Apocrypha as part of the New Testament, not so Protestants. For Protestants the Bible is all you need to know about what is wanted by God. Catholics think their chief - the Pope - is an infallible representative of God; the successor to Peter and Vicar of Christ, so anything he, the Pope, says goes. In other words, Papal edicts act as if additional, interpretative rules to those of the Bible. Protestants do not have the equivalent of a Papal figurehead.

There are other lesser differences; here's what Catholics have and not so Protestants:

Requiring all the faithful to attend Mass (formal church service) each Sunday and on Holy days, Christmas (Christ's birthday) and Easter (Christ's resurrection). Protestants are encouraged but not absolutely required to attend church;
Prayer to saints and the Virgin Mary;
The confessional;
Priestly celibacy and unwedded priests;
The Rosary, a bead prayer counter.

Then there is the Catholic purgatory, although there is no mention of this in the Bible. This is a place for those who were pretty good in life but not quite up to the standard of goodliness required for immediate entry to heaven. After purification they can get to the level of holiness required to pass through the pearly gates. This purgatory is a bit like the Jewish Gehinnom, where souls are cleansed before entry to heaven. Sometimes purgatory may be seen as synonymous with "Limbo of the Fathers"; a medieval term. Protestants don't have a purgatory, this in-between afterlife location. For them you either go straight up to heaven or straight down to hell.

Another theological claim by Catholicism is that Limbo is for babies who are too young to have been branded with 'original sin'. In this version of Limbo, unbaptised babies, who die in infancy, cannot go to heaven, they reside in a place called Limbo. Poor parents, they never get to be with them in the afterlife. However, the Catholic church took pity on such parents in 2007. This was when Pope Benedict XVI endorsed a 41-page document which expunged the centuries old Limbo. But the Catholic church did not want to give up entirely on the idea that baptism was an essential route to heaven. Limbo was replaced with hope: "There are reasons to hope that God will save those infants precisely because it was not possible (to baptize them).", seems a fudge.

In addition to the Bible, Catholics, usually known as Roman Catholics, have a series of entrenched traditions, often the result of Papal proclamations. These liturgical practices (wide ranging public rites), for Catholics, are founded on the seven sacraments (specific holy rites, inspired by Christ):

Modern view of the Bible

Anyone reading the Bible and trying to interpret it within the context of our current level of understanding will have trouble accepting its purported Godly truths. We have much more general knowledge than our forefathers. There are things we know of the world; it's history; geography, cosmology and science generally that provide explanations not previously known. Prime examples are:

what we now know about evolution, through work started by Darwin and via the fossil record;
the age and development of the Earth, through Geological studies and cosmology has given us a much deeper understanding of the enormous size, age and complexity of the universe.

This wealth of modern knowledge helps lift the veil of mystery and superstition that underpins much of the Bible. It casts grave doubts on of the validity of much Biblical text. We know that the cosmos is about 13.8 billion years old. It was not formed and populated with the first humans in seven days, as claimed by the Genesis chapter of the Bible. The Earth is not at its centre. It is one tiny planet, 4.6 billion years old, among many billions of others. Our Earth is not 5,650 years old, as Bishop Ussher, in the 17th century, calculated from the Bible. We know that humans evolved rather than were formed directly by God. These are just a few of the many issues with the Bible that our modern knowledge and understanding throw up.

Justification for the Bible

Given all the above, how do people manage to remain believers in the Bible? For some there is no problem. They have simple, unquestioning acceptance - faith. A few might claim divine revelation. Others less certain might say, "Well, I'll keep an open mind and some of it makes sense to me." Certainly, it's a choice between religions, humanism and atheism which are not easy to make. How do they manage this given all the vagaries that exist? They certainly have to perform some mental gymnastics. Their common, although unstated approach, to justifying the Bible, appears to be made through one or other of these processes, which may be entirely unconscious:

Selective amnesia;
Dismissive choice;
Comfortable familiarity;
Creative interpretation.

Selective amnesia

Through this method the really bad bits are pushed to the back of the cranial backyard. These have to fight hard to get any serious consideration.

Women are required to marry and bear children;
Wives must be obedient to their husbands, although are equal before God;
Men may divorce their wives without cause and retain sole custody rights over children who have reached a certain age;
In some court proceedings a woman's testament is considered inferior, half that of a man's, and a sister can only inherit half that of her brother. Qur'anic law assumes the husband will maintain the sister;
Women may only be in the company of other women or close male relatives;
Men may be polygamous and marry up to four women. A woman may marry only one man;
Women are required to dress modestly and may not reveal some parts of their body to other than their husband. Some Muslims interpret this strictly by requiring women to wear the burqa, which covers the entire body and face, with mesh in front of the eyes. Others use the niqab, which leaves the eyes uncovered. At the bottom end of the scale is use of the hijab, a simple head scarf, leaving the whole face uncovered.
By law, this is a requirement in Iran and some other Muslim dominated countries. There is no definitive, common agreement among Muslim scholars on what, exactly is required of women's dress to achieve the Qur'anic requirement of modesty;
Men do not need to cover their faces, heads or upper body;
Women may not pray or touch the Qur'an during menstruation or for a time following childbirth. They are considered to be ritually impure;
Women may now pray in mosques but must be separate from men. For several centuries mosques were a male preserve;
Muslim families commonly arrange marriage of their daughters and sons. Daughters usually have no say and are sometimes forced, against their will, into an unwanted marriage;
Here are a couple of Qur'anic verses concerning women:
Qur'an 2:224 - "Your women are a tillage for you; so come unto your tillage as you wish......"
Qur'an 4:35 - "Men are the managers of affairs of women..... Righteous women are therefore obedient.....And those you fear may be rebellious admonish; banish them to their couches and beat them. If they obey you look not for any way against them......."

Muslim societies have nearly always been exclusively patriarchal and male dominated. It's extremely rare for women to be allowed to reach any position of prominence in the 1400 years of Islam, i.e. since Mohammed. Benazir Bhutto bucked the trend by becoming Prime Minister of Pakistan, in 1983. A suicide bomber later assassinated her. Some sources say this was Taliban inspired. Tansu Ciller became Turkey's Prime Minister in 1993. This was a period of change following Kemal Ataturk, the great reformer. He was a transformational Turkish leader following the collapse of the Ottoman Empire. President Ataturk relaxed some strict Islamic cultural rules and gave women equal civil and political rights.

Progress for Women

In many areas of employment and personal freedom Muslim women have been severely constrained in most Islamic societies. Saudi Arabia only allowed women to drive cars for the first time in 2018. Gaining higher education has been a problem for girls in most Muslim dominated countries. Sometimes, any form of education for girls has been at issue. Famously, Malala Yousafzai went against the Taliban's ban on girl's education in Afghanistan. As a consequence, she was shot in the head in 2013. Later she received the Nobel Peace Prize for championing the right of Muslim girls to receive an education. Malala was fortunate to be strongly supported by her father. He was determined to see his daughter educated, free to choose her own way and not be forced into marriage at a very young age.

Honour Killings

It can be very dangerous for Muslim women to refuse an arranged marriage, or to leave one. In the UK, it's estimated that up to 12 Muslim women a year were killed in so-called 'honour' killings in the first decade of the 21st century. Usually the murderer is the father, and/or other male relative. This is claimed to be mostly a cultural issue, not an Islamic one. Some Muslims clearly think this action is allowable within their religion. Questions could be asked about this such as: How is it honourable to take away a daughter/relative's choice and brutally kill her, and where in the Qur'an is this practice approved? Are Qur'an 2:224 and 4:35 used as justification?

Challenging the ideology that supports terrorism;
Protecting vulnerable people;
Supporting sectors and institutions where there are risks of radicalisation.

The vast majority of Muslims in Britain are peaceful. But, the hard evidence clearly shows that the words of the Muslim Council conference and various Imams have either not been universally listened to, or not believed.

Nor has the Prevent Strategy been entirely successful, although it's likely to have foiled a number of potential attacks. It failed to stop Khairi Saadallah, a Libyan asylum seeker, stabbing 3 men to death in a Reading park in June 2020. He also tried to kill 3 others. Witnesses said Saadallah shouted "Allahu Akbar" - God is the greatest - during his deadly rampage. He showed that some Muslims in Britain and elsewhere continue to have no problem in engaging in deadly violence in the name of Islam. Islam was widely established 1,400 years ago in violence, this has continued sporadically through the centuries up to the present day. Can it be said that Islam is a religion of peace? As the old English saying goes: "The proof of the pudding is in the eating."

Baroness Sayeeda Warsi's Perspective

Any God that we can personally design will be quite different from previous models. Our God's universe will be wholly fun rather than holy glum. God's default state will be constantly friendly and sympathetic towards humanity - no killing earthquakes, tidal waves, hurricanes, droughts or other harmful climatic events. No wars; diseases; injuries or famines either.

HE will also arrange things in such a way so that a line is drawn over which humans cannot step to do hurt to others, themselves. or indeed to harm our beautiful planet. "Cruelty" and "suffering" will become redundant words and expunged from every dictionary. Specific parameters will be set down, in the form of a contract describing what humans want of God and, conversely, what God requires of humans.

First, let's look at what the new God needs of humans in the contract. Just like Isaac Asimov's "three laws of robotics" there will be "three laws of God", which humans will feel compelled to adhere to. Well, of course there will always be a lot more than three laws, otherwise governments and parliaments would suffer extreme deprivation symptoms and lawyers would be fewer and poorer. But our three laws will be fundamental, basic core laws representing the divine will. They will apply to all humans at all times and all where. These are the statutes of our customised God, HE Requires All humans to:

Be fair to and help their fellow man or woman. If you treat others fairly and helpfully they are more likely to reciprocate and everyone will be happier;

Take an interest in a wide range of things. It's an amazing world out there, find out as much about it and the wider universe as you can. The more you know, the better the life decisions you will make. Wider knowledge is also the antipathy of rigid, dogmatic thinking and actions;

Look after the world they have inherited. It's a moral imperative that we should hand it on to future generations in good, enjoyable and utilisable shape.

Key features of the proposed God specification will be drawn up in a White, Black, Yellow and Brown Paper. This discriminatory free, consultative document will be subject to a series of votes by every Earth person over the age of 10 (children have a say in this too).

Here are the God Commissioning Committee members:

Chairman: Archangel Gabriel, Celestial. Archie Gabriel has close-up, first-hand knowledge of a God, warts and all. He is also the guardian of "truth", which can be something of a double-edged sword. Hence, he wields one of these. Gabriel, with his personalised transportation plumage, is never held up by traffic problems. He will therefore set a good example by always being on time for committee meetings. Of course, his limited golfing prowess is a bit of handicap for a senior committee man, with so many decisions commonly taken on the golf course, or in the club bar. But being a wimp on the links should not detract too much. He's certain to have the right balls for this job;

Secretary: William Shakespeare, English. Bill is someone who has the most extreme ability to convey the written word in a timeless fashion to the whole world. He will draw up reports giving the committee's reasoning and conclusions for all Earth to see. Most importantly, he will draft a moral guide book on behalf of our new God. This will commonly be found unread and in pristine condition in every hotel bedroom on Earth. It will be called "The Babble";

Assistant Secretary: Charles Dickens, English. Like Bill, Charlie is also pretty handy with a pen. His particular skill is to convey the triumph of goodness and philanthropy in mesmeric prose - remember Oliver Twist and a Christmas Carol among others. Charlie will help temporise the danger of any dark and difficult Macbethian; Hamletian or Othelloian writing style that Bill may be tempted to employ;

Treasurer: David Hume, Scottish. Dave brings a lot to the party. He is a philosopher, historian and an economist. Once the new God is in place, there is no one better than Dave to say exactly what God is, although he won't be so hot in speculating what God ought to be. In his time (the 18th century) he expressed doubts about the common proofs for God's, i.e. the Christian one's, existence. He also pooh-poohed the link between morality and religion and he scoffed at miracles. These attributes will be useful. His renowned scepticism is ameliorated by a strong streak of realism. God will clearly have to be plugged into a viable, realistic, historical context. Empirical Dave will certainly cause this to happen. In addition, Dave's pragmatic approach will help, together with Socs, (see below) ensure the committee don't get too carried away into esoteric flights of fancy. He will also have the specific job of keeping an eye on the committee's budget, as Treasurer. Scots are meant to be good with money. Hopefully the almost totally credit crunched Scottish banks RBS and HBOS of 2009 are not typical of Scottish financial acumen;

Head of Marketing: St. Paul (reverting to Saul) Paul's letters to several different and mysterious "ians" i.e. Corinthians, Galatians, Philippians etc. - curiously, none of the ians came from Scotland - have been read out in churches all over the world for thousands of years. He certainly knows how to get a message across to the masses. Never mind that his past thoughts and writings on topics such as an early version of "God the divine watchmaker"; humanity's "original sin"; and "the end of the world is nigh" have put forward distinctly dodgy propositions or predictions, that is to say: they were Wholly, with a capital W, in error. And his views on women will make for interesting committee banter with Mary W., a fearsome feminist. No matter, all that's needed to set Paul on a new, enlightened path is a another very bright light in the sky followed by a little bit of re-education. It will then be a new dawn for Paul. To signify his re-branding Paul will revert to his original name - Saul. The reinvented Saul will be able to mix it fabulously with the best of the theological and philosophical luvvies;

Other Committee members:

Socrates, Greek. Socs will talk to anyone about anything. He asks endless, forensically probing questions and through this technique strips away the veneer of hypocrisy, prejudice and misunderstanding so that people arrive closer to their own truth;

Epicurus, This ancient Greek philosopher will insist committee meetings are held in the garden. The natural beauty of the plants and outdoor scenery will help foster a calm, relaxed attitude. Committee members, will be encouraged to make the most of every day and ensure the new God supports the natural environment, the pursuit of happiness and explains why not to fear death;

Isaac Newton and Albert Einstein, English and German. Zac and Bert were two of the greatest scientific minds of all time. They will ensure the new God has a firm scientific, provable base. Too long has God sat, in most religions, at the frontiers of science. Quite often the religious devout have found it necessary to ignore, dismiss or bury uncomfortable scientific facts. Sometimes, they've buried both the facts and the bearer of them. We won't have any of that nonsense with our new God. Zac and Bert will help design a God who is within, not outside, scientific boundaries. He will be a God who can be fitted comfortably within a new, Great Theory of Everything (also known as the Big Toe theory). Zac will bring to these considerations his gravitas and Bert his relatives;

